# Fleet choices about the mission itself, and the approach mystery thread.
# Arc and plants: content/STORY_BIBLE.md (spoilers). Voice: content/STYLE.md.

card dec_halfway
type decision
cat mission
days 19 23
weight 14
once
duration 1
roles A B
lean warm feast quiet
text Halfway. Earth and Mars are the same distance behind and ahead, give or take a rounding error. {A} wants to break out the good rations and mark it properly. {B} would rather nobody counted out loud yet.
option feast | Mark it | The fleet eats the good rations on seven screens at once. {A} gives a toast about the halfway mark, and it turns into a toast about the people who packed the food.
  res food -2
  morale A 4
  morale B 2
option quiet | Let it pass | Halfway passes without a word on the fleet channel. On every ship, at the same minute, someone looks at the clock anyway.
  bond A B 3

card dec_name
type decision
cat mission
days 26 34
weight 10
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious after home
text The imaging pass has turned up an unnamed crater near the landing zone, small enough that the fleet gets to suggest a name. {A} wants to name it after the first person who ever wrote a Mars mission plan. {B} wants to name it after somewhere at home.
option after | Honor the planners | The name goes in the request. Somewhere, a committee will argue about it for years, which {A} considers part of the honor.
  trait A curious 2
option home | Name it for home | The crater gets the name of a small town on Earth that nobody outside it has heard of. Within a day, the town's newspaper has a front page about it.
  trait B warm 3
  morale B 4

card dec_science
type decision
cat mission
days 14 30
weight 8
once
duration 2
roles A B
lean curious record save
text A gamma-ray burst lights up the sky somewhere past the galaxy, and the fleet is the farthest instrument humanity has. Holding attitude for the measurement costs propellant. {A} wants the data. {B} wants the propellant.
option record | Hold for the data | Seven ships hold still for twenty minutes, and a burst that left its source before Earth had oceans is triangulated from a baseline nobody has had before. {A} reads the confirmation twice.
  res fuel -2
  trait A curious 3
  morale A 3
option save | Keep the propellant | The fleet passes. Ground telescopes get the burst anyway, from one angle. {B} logs the propellant saved, and doesn't pretend it was an easy trade.
  trait B brave 2

# --- Night-side flashes (part 1-4) -------------------------------------------------
card m_flash1
type event
cat mystery
days 27 32
weight 14
once
roles A
when cond A ok
text On the dark limb of Mars, through the window scope, {A} sees a pinprick of light. Gone. Then again. {A} times it out of habit, writes the number down, and doesn't go back to bed.
do set flash_seen
do mark t_flash
do trait A curious 2

card m_flash2
type event
cat mystery
days 30 35
weight 30
once
roles A
when flag flash_seen
when since t_flash >= 2
when cond A ok
text {A} points the scope at the same patch of night, at the same hour. The flash is there, same rhythm, a little brighter. {A} wakes the rest of the ship. Nobody whispers, but everyone talks a little more quietly.
do set flash_twice

card dec_flash
type decision
cat mystery
days 32 38
weight 40
once
duration 1
roles A B
when flag flash_twice
lean curious look leave
text The flashes on the dark side are still there. {A} wants to slew the fleet's best camera onto them before capture, at a cost in propellant. {B} wants to leave them for the people who'll be down there with time to look.
option look | Look closer | The camera swings. {A} sets up a long exposure and doesn't look away from the screen until it finishes.
  set flash_looked
  res fuel -1
option leave | Leave it for the ground | The camera stays where it is. The flashes go into the anomaly log, under a heading {B} writes and then underlines.
  set flash_left
  morale A -2

card flash_answer
type event
cat mystery
clue
due
once
days 34 39
roles A
when flag flash_looked
when since t_flash >= 4
when cond A ok
text The exposure comes back. The flash isn't in orbit. It's on the ground: one point of light at the edge of the landing zone, keeping time. {A} checks the coordinates twice, then goes to wake the others.
do trait A curious 4
do morale A 2

card m_overlay
type event
cat mystery
clue
due
once
days 37 39
roles A
when fired m_flash2
when fired dec_signal
when cond A ok
text {A} lays the old dish log over the scope video, frame by frame, because nobody else has had time. The carrier peaks on the exact frame the flash does. Every time. {A} prints both traces and pins them up side by side.
do trait A curious 3

# --- Middle-dark plant (a careful reader's detail; ordinary explanations exist) ---
card m_ripple
type event
cat mystery
clue
days 13 24
weight 6
once
roles A
when cond A ok
text {A} cleans up the dish noise floor for the weekly report and finds a faint ripple in it, period about eleven seconds. Reaction wheel harmonic, most likely. {A} notes it in the margin and moves on to the propellant tables.
do trait A curious 1

# --- The crew's theory (misdirection; see STORY_BIBLE.md) -------------------------
card m_theory
type event
cat mystery
days 26 33
weight 14
once
roles A
when fired dec_signal
when cond A ok
text {A} works it out on the galley whiteboard: a dead orbiter, tumbling, its antenna sweeping past the fleet once a turn. Eleven seconds a rotation. It fits. {A} draws the orbit in blue marker, and for the first time in a while the whole ship sleeps through the night.
do morale A 3

# --- The payoff: fires on day 40 in every season ------------------------------------
card m_reveal
type event
cat mystery
due
once
days 40 41
roles A
text On the last imaging pass, {A} finds the flash on the map grid. It's standing at the edge of the landing zone, on its legs, tilted a few degrees: Cargo 2. Every eleven seconds, its lights blink once. {A} reads the timestamp and doesn't say anything for a while.
do trait A curious 3
